{"course": {"schema_version": "1.0", "course_id": "mitocw-17-523-fall-2005", "institution": "Massachusetts Institute of Technology", "platform": "MIT OpenCourseWare", "course_codes": ["17.523"], "cross_listed_codes": [], "course_title": "Ethnicity and Race in World Politics", "course_slug": "17-523-ethnicity-and-race-in-world-politics-fall-2005", "term": "Fall", "year": 2005, "as_taught_in": "Fall 2005", "language": "en", "departments": ["Political Science"], "academic_levels": ["Undergraduate"], "instructors": ["Prof. Melissa Nobles"], "subject_primary": "Philosophy", "subject_secondary": ["Political Philosophy", "Social Science", "Anthropology", "Ethnography", "Social Anthropology", "Political Science", "International Relations", "Sociology", "Social Justice"], "topic_tree": [{"name": "Humanities", "subtopics": [{"name": "Philosophy", "specialties": ["Political Philosophy"]}]}, {"name": "Social Science", "subtopics": [{"name": "Anthropology", "specialties": ["Ethnography", "Social Anthropology"]}, {"name": "Political Science", "specialties": ["International Relations"]}, {"name": "Sociology", "specialties": ["Social Justice"]}]}], "topic_keywords": [], "pedagogy_tags": [], "assessment_types": [], "course_feature_tags": ["Lecture Notes", "Written Assignments with Examples"], "description": "Discerning the ethnic and racial dimensions of politics is considered by some indispensable to understanding contemporary world politics. This course seeks to answer fundamental questions about racial and ethnic politics. To begin, what are the bases of ethnic and racial identities? What accounts for political mobilization based upon such identities? What are the political claims and goals of such mobilization and is conflict between groups and/or with government forces inevitable? How do ethnic and racial identities intersect with other identities, such as gender and class, which are themselves the sources of social, political, and economic cleavages? Finally, how are domestic 